A||N||13||Monkeyman||Problems with the new brakes?????||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||09-19-2000||03:44 AM||||I've had my new GA/Beretta brakes for a few weeks now.  Not bad but I just noticed yesterday that there is a surge in them.  With a light pedal, going slow I can feel them surge badly.  All I can figure is warped rotors, but I haven't hit the brakes hard even once and they're brand new.  Any other suggestions I can look into?<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||09-19-2000||09:26 AM||||were they new disc's that you put on? it you have access to the tools, there's a handy little depth guage that attaches to the caliper and basically has a little needle that measures the distance to the rotor, as you turn the rotor, any warps or bulges will show up as changes in distance. or if you can 'feel' which wheel it is (does the car pull to either side when it surges?) you could try just figuring out which rotor it is, pull that one and take it down to mineky or some other break house to get it turned down.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||09-19-2000||12:31 PM||||too wierd.  this morning, i was thinking to myself... "myself, i wounder how MM is doing with his new brake's?"<P>and myself said "we need to ask him."<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>ps<BR>sounds like a warped rotor, or a dragging piston.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||09-19-2000||03:55 PM||||The thicker vented GA rotors are not as prone to warpage as the old Fiero ones, but they still will warp under the right (or is it the wrong) conditions. A rotor warping is often caused improper lug nut torquing or excessive heat ( like a dragging caliper). You did use a torque wrench and the correct torquing sequence, right?<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 09-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-19-2000||06:12 PM||||A torque wrench on my lug nuts??  I didn't know that I needed to do that.  Maybe that's what caused whatever problem I'm having.  Darn, drat, shoot, bummer, etc.  If one of them is warped already, I'll probably exchange it for a new one.  (I hope.)  BTW--It pulls slightly to the right.  I'm hoping this is just due to a little bit of brake fluid on the left front rotor.  It did this before I noticed the shuddering.<P>I've been trying to find the time to finish the brake job.  All that's left is the SS braided lines.  I just don't have the time to do those, so I'll probably just have the shop take care of that.  I pulled my dash out of the car a few weeks ago planning to start on a custom dash/center console but haven't really had the time to do much with that, either.  Someday...<P>BTW--Nice to see you back, bb.||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||09-19-2000||07:58 PM||||ditto, batboy!<P>MM, lug nuts should be torqued to... i beleieve it is 80lbs.<P>i will check in the morning, dark now... and i cant see the reading on the wrench.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||09-19-2000||08:19 PM||||Torqueing lug nuts!?!?!?!?!?!? you guys are kidding right? never in my life have I used a torque wrench on lugs, only had one warped rotor, and it was old as hell when I got it (my old '76 Lemans).||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||09-19-2000||08:32 PM||||Lugs are 100 ft-lbs||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||09-19-2000||08:51 PM||||After I take the Grape Ape to the shop for the brake lines, I'll rent a torque wrench again and make sure that the torque is correct.  I hadn't heard that before so I didn't know that I had to do it.  Something odd I noticed the last time I removed my wheels...it looks like my wheels were made for those lug nuts that just look like a long sleeve as opposed to the stock kind that are beveled.  I haven't noticed any problems so far, but I might stop by the local tire shop and pick up a set of the right kind of lug nuts.  I appreciate the help.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-19-2000||10:59 PM||||did you clean the new rotors? The last thing you should have done before putting the calipers and pads in place was clean them. If there was any grease on them at all then you can get this kind of problem. Grease tracked in on the pad faces could do it to.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||09-20-2000||05:18 AM||||I guess you learn something new everyday.  (Today I learned that I'm not even qualified to open a can of beer, let alone work on a car.)<P>Ogre--I had no idea I was supposed to do that.  If this is what's causing my problem, is it temporary?  Do I need to replace the pads (again--already replaced them once due to a slight brake fluid leak)?||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||09-20-2000||09:36 AM||||Clean the rotors. then take fine sand paper to them to remove any stuck material. then clean again. (An random orbital sander held flat on the surface is best for this.)<P>You can try taking some sand paper to the pads. put the paper on something smooth hard and flat then hold the pad to sand.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||09-20-2000||05:06 PM||||Without looking up the torque numbers for the lug nuts, I believe the manual says 80 ft/lbs for steel wheels and 100 ft/lbs for the alloy wheels. You SHOULD torque the lug nuts to specs on every disc brake vehicle. Trust me, rotors will will warp if they aren't tightened down evenly. What I do first is to run one lug down to about 60 ft/lbs, skip one lug and then tighten the next, etc. until all five are done. Then I do the same for 80 ft/lbs, then again at the final torque reading. This gradually and evenly tightens all the lugs so there is no chance of warpage.<P>Monkey, just save up a few bucks and buy a torque wrench. I don't know how a guy can work on a car nowadays without one. I saw a cheapie but serviceable click-stop torque wrench on sale at O'Reilly's a few months ago for only $20.<P>By the way, the rotor probably isn't warped bad, just have it turned the minumim amount needed to clean it back up and you're back in business. What Ogre is describing with very fine sandpaper is a way to deglaze the rotor after machining (rotors are still turned even when new). I personally never do this, but I do make sure there is nothing (like dirt and grease) on the rotor as I'm installing them. Even "clean" fingers will leave an oily film on the surface. There is special brake cleaner solvent made for this, but I just use a little rubbing alcohol and a lint-free rag.||||reg||1
Z||000013||rockcrawl||09-20-2000||05:23 PM||||I always laugh at my buddy when he gets out the torque wrench to tighten down lugs. I've never torqued lug nuts. I just tighten them down to about three grunts, and that does it. But en-even lug nut torque can cause warped rotors.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
